{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user (via the cobbler-scaffold orchestrator) has assigned task #5548: implement cmd/chmod verbose, silent, and recursive options corresponding to srd089 requirements R2.1, R2.2, R2.3, and R2.4. Specifically:\n   - R2.1: `-R` / `--recursive` flag for recursive directory traversal\n   - R2.2: `-v` / `--verbose` flag printing a diagnostic for every file processed\n   - R2.3: `-c` / `--changes` flag printing a diagnostic only when a file's mode actually changed\n   - R2.4: `-f` / `--silent` / `--quiet` flag suppressing most error messages\n   \n   The only file to modify is `cmd/chmod/main.go`. Design decision D1 mandates using `filepath.WalkDir` for recursive traversal.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils chmod\n   - Differential testing against `gchmod` (Homebrew GNU reference binary)\n   - Symbolic and octal mode parsing (already implemented in R1.x)\n   - `filepath.WalkDir` for recursive directory traversal\n   - SIGPIPE handling via `pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n   - Execution constitution: 40-line function limit (E8), 500-line file limit (E9), no git commands, write all files before building\n   - Copyright header required on every Go file\n   - No scope creep (E3) — only modify `cmd/chmod/main.go`\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd089-chmod.yaml`\n     - Read to understand exact requirement text for R2.1-R2.4\n     - Key requirements: R2.1 recursive, R2.2 verbose (every file), R2.3 changes (only changed files), R2.4 silent/quiet (suppress errors)\n     - Non-goals: no SELinux, no --preserve-root\n   \n   - `cmd/chmod/main.go` (existing, 306 lines)\n     - Already implements R1.1-R1.4 (mode parsing: octal + symbolic), R4.1-R4.3 (exit codes, SIGPIPE)\n     - Help text already lists -c, -f, -v, -R flags but they are NOT parsed or implemented\n     - `parseArgs` function (line 57-87): Currently does NOT handle any flags (-c, -f, -v, -R). It treats first non-flag arg as mode, rest as files. Only handles `--help`, `--version`, `--`.\n     - `main()` (line 35-55): Iterates over files calling `applyMode`, prints errors to stderr\n     - `applyMode` (line 89-94): Dispatches to `applyOctalMode` or `applySymbolicMode`\n     - `applyOctalMode` (line 112-118): Parses octal, calls `os.Chmod`\n     - `applySymbolicMode` (line 126-137): Stats file, calls `evalSymbolic`, calls `os.Chmod`\n     - `evalSymbolic` (line 139-149): Processes comma-separated symbolic clauses\n     - `evalClause` (line 151-167): Parses who+op+perms within a single clause\n     - `permBits` struct (line 169-177): Holds parsed permission flags\n     - `parseWho` (line 179-190): Extracts ugoa prefix\n     - `parsePerms` (line 192-216): Extracts rwxXst permission bits\n     - `applyOp` (line 218-242): Applies +/-/= operation with umask handling\n     - `buildMask` (line 244-281): Builds permission mask for a specific who target\n     - `applyEquals` (line 283-298): Handles = operator by clearing then setting\n     - `formatErr` (line 300-305): Formats PathError for display\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet — only read operations were performed before the summary was requested.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Identified that `parseArgs` needs to be refactored to handle flag parsing (-c, -f, -v, -R and their long forms)\n   - Need to add state variables (verbose, changes, silent, recursive booleans)\n   - Need to modify `main()` to use `filepath.WalkDir` when recursive is set\n   - Need to add diagnostic output formatting (showing old mode → new mode) for verbose/changes modes\n   - Need to suppress errors in silent mode\n   - Must keep functions under 40 lines per E8\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - 